<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sturmi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 705–<a href="/years/779">779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Sturm, Sturmius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sturmi, or Sturm, was a Bavarian-born disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> and the founder and first abbot of the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, established in 744. He made Fulda a model Benedictine house and took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s mission among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. He was canonised in 1139.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sturmi.jpg" alt="Sturmi" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Larenz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Mark_Notgeld_1921_Stadt_Marsberg_No_005588_Ausschnitt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sturm was most likely related to the Agilolfing dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His parents placed him in the care of Boniface, who was then reorganising the church in Bavaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and he was educated at the Benedictine monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> under Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wigbert">Wigbert</a>. As a missionary in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> he founded a monastic settlement at Haerulfisfeld (Hersfeld) in 736, was ordained priest at Fritzlar in 740 and spent three years as a missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. He then lived as a hermit at Hersfeld until raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> drove him from his unprotected hermitage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 744 Boniface sent him to found a monastery in the region of Eichloha, granted to Boniface by the mayor of the palace Carloman. Sturm built it at a ford on the Fulda River, in the ruins of a 6th-century Merovingian royal camp destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Around 748 he and two other monks spent a year studying Benedictine life as practised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a> placed Fulda under the jurisdiction of the Vatican rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. After Boniface was killed near Dokkum in 754, Sturm secured his burial at Fulda against the bishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and Utrecht, and the abbey became a major place of pilgrimage.</p><p>The exemption led to serious conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullus">Lullus</a>, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, who in 763 persuaded Pepin to banish Sturm to Jumièges in Normandy. The monks refused the abbot Lull named, were allowed to elect their own, and within two years won Sturm's return. In 774 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> under royal protection and assigned it mission territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, part of the Frankish effort to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> lands and impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> by force. Sturm founded the abbey of St. Boniface at Hamelin, but when Charlemagne left to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the Saxons revolted and drove out the monks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 779 Sturm went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, fell ill, and died soon after returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, on 17 December 779; he was buried there. His life was written in the Vita Sturmi by Eigil of Fulda, the fourth abbot, a relative who had been a monk there for over 20 years under him. Recognised as a saint before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-west-schism">East–West Schism</a> of 1054, he is also honoured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>. Saint Sturm's Fountain in front of the old town hall of Fulda shows him with Benedict and Boniface.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Sturm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sturm</b>, a disciple of Saint Boniface, founded the monastery at Fulda in 744, and Boniface was buried there in 754.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-fulda">Prince-Abbey of Fulda</a></li><li><blockquote>Hersfeld began as a hermitage of Saint <b>Sturmi</b> around 736 and was refounded as a Benedictine monastery by Archbishop Lullus of Mainz around 769.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hersfeld">Principality of Hersfeld</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Enns, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey">Fulda monastery</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.22,14.48&amp;year=753" aria-label="Enns on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sturmi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5816 5911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5816 5911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.22,14.48&amp;year=753">What was here in 753 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
